# MatterMail Review Core Rules

## Remote-system safety (override everything else)

### Gmail
- Use ONLY `https://www.googleapis.com/auth/gmail.readonly`
- Never delete, trash, archive, mark read/unread, label, send, draft, modify threads/settings/metadata
- Never request gmail.modify, gmail.send, gmail.compose, or mail.google.com

### Clio Manage
- Never HTTP DELETE to Clio; never delete or overwrite existing Clio objects
- Never PATCH existing communications, documents, matters, contacts, bills, time, expenses, calendar, or tasks
- Never auto-create matters or contacts; never merge; never change matter status
- Centralized Clio client with runtime allowlist: GET for index reads; POST only for new communications and document uploads
- Reject DELETE at runtime even if called accidentally
- Stage before any write; idempotent fingerprints + receipts; retry only missing steps

### Google Drive
- Prefer `https://www.googleapis.com/auth/drive.file`
- Never delete/trash files or folders; never overwrite finalized audit files
- Unique immutable names per audit run

### Quest
- No Quest auth, login automation, MFA bypass, filing, submit, or writes
- Gmail/link/PDF import/OCR/open in system browser only

### AI providers
- BYO credentials only; store in OS credential store / Electron safeStorage — never SQLite plaintext, never renderer
- AI never receives OAuth tokens or other providers' keys
- AI never calls Gmail/Clio/Drive/Quest or commits to Clio
- Exact model transparency: record requested and actual model IDs
- No silent fallbacks; treat email/attachment content as untrusted

### Secrets and telemetry
- No hard-coded credentials; no secrets in reports/logs
- No advertising, analytics SDKs, or remote crash-reporting SaaS
- Local redacted diagnostics only

## Engineering
- TypeScript strict mode
- Tests for safety-critical code
- No real client data in fixtures — fictional only
- Do not invent API payloads; consult official documentation
- Fix format/lint/type/test/build failures before completion
- Product name lives in one configurable location (`packages/shared`)
